# Question: with the throughput the team has really had, how many weeks will
# the rest of the backlog take, and how likely is it to be done within 12
# weeks?
#
# Run it with the example throughput below:
#   ubc scenarios/delivery/delivery-date.ubc
# Use your own history, one number per line (items finished each week):
#   ubc --data throughput=weeks.txt scenarios/delivery/delivery-date.ubc
# Change an assumption, for example a backlog that has been trimmed:
#   ubc --set 'backlog = 30 to 40' scenarios/delivery/delivery-date.ubc
# See which assumption matters most:
#   ubc --sens weeks scenarios/delivery/delivery-date.ubc
#
# Every "a to b" is a 90% confidence interval. A list "[...]" is observed data:
# every sample picks one of its values at random.

# Assumptions
backlog = 40 to 60                # items left, splitting and new findings included
throughput = [3, 5, 2, 6, 4, 4, 7, 3, 5, 1, 4, 6]   # items finished each of the last 12 weeks; --data replaces it
deadline = 12                     # weeks until the date we were asked for

# Calculation
horizon = mean(backlog) / mean(throughput)                                 # rough number of weeks ahead
weekly_rate = normal(mean(throughput), stdev(throughput) / sqrt(horizon))  # average of that many weeks: good and bad weeks even out
weeks = backlog / weekly_rate                                              # weeks to finish the backlog

# Result
weeks
P(weeks <= deadline)              # how likely is the backlog to be done within 12 weeks?
